  "content": {
    "id": "p26-q43-content",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "43",
    "prompt": "Let $f(x)=k(e^x)+ax+b$, where $k$, $a$ and $b$ are constants with $k\\ne0$, and $e$ is Euler's number.\n\nShow that $k$ and $a$ must have opposite signs in order for the graph of $y=f(x)$ to have a stationary point.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": {
        "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
        "version": 1,
        "blocks": [
          {
            "id": "p26-q43-short-condition",
            "type": "paragraph",
            "fontSize": null,
            "align": "left",
            "spaceBefore": 0,
            "spaceAfter": 2,
            "lineHeight": 1.4,
            "indent": 0,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "type": "math",
                "latex": "f'(x)=ke^x+a=0\\quad\\text{requires}\\quad-\\frac ak>0.",
                "display": false
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "p26-q43-short-conclusion",
            "type": "paragraph",
            "fontSize": null,
            "align": "left",
            "spaceBefore": 0,
            "spaceAfter": 2,
            "lineHeight": 1.4,
            "indent": 0,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Since ",
                "marks": []
              },
              {
                "type": "math",
                "latex": "e^x>0",
                "display": false
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": ", ",
                "marks": []
              },
              {
                "type": "math",
                "latex": "k",
                "display": false
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": " and ",
                "marks": []
              },
              {
                "type": "math",
                "latex": "a",
                "display": false
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": " must have opposite signs.",
                "marks": []
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      "worked": "$$f'(x)=ke^x+a.$$\n\nAt a stationary point,\n\n$$\\begin{align*}ke^x+a&=0\\\\ke^x&=-a\\\\e^x&=-\\frac ak.\\end{align*}$$\n\nSince $e^x>0$,\n\n$$-\\frac ak>0.$$\n\nThus $\\frac{a}{k}<0$, so $k$ and $a$ must have opposite signs. When they do, the stationary point occurs at $x=\\ln\\left(-\\frac ak\\right)$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
